    {
      "id": "d85ba3927717",
      "name": "Slack business executive brief",
      "prompt": "You are writing DJ Mauch's single smart business briefing for Flow. The pre-run script injects (1) the current Daily Top of Mind active list, (2) lightweight bounded Gmail context from personal + 166-2nd inboxes, (3) rolling topics from prior briefs, and (4) latest Slack collection evidence including channels, DMs, and MPIMs.\n\nUse the Daily ToM list as the priority lens for reviewing both Slack and email. The email context is intentionally light: bounded recent searches and ToM-keyword matches only, not a full mailbox crawl.\n\nOutput one integrated briefing only \u2014 do not create separate Slack/email/ToM briefings. Structure:\n1. Top alerts / decisions for DJ\n2. ToM-linked updates: cite relevant ToM item(s), whether the signal came from Slack or email, what happened, who said it/sent it, why it matters, and DJ action\n3. Other important Slack/email items not already on ToM\n4. Suggested ToM additions (approval required): list candidate items only; do not write to the ToM doc or imply they were added\n5. Watchlist / follow-ups\n\nRules:\n- Never write to the Daily ToM doc.\n- Never send/reply/forward Slack or Gmail messages.\n- Never mark Gmail read/unread, archive, label, or modify email.\n- Prefer concise executive synthesis over raw transcript/email dumps.\n- Include enough context for DJ to understand the issue without opening Slack/Gmail.\n- MPIM/group-DM coverage is required; if evidence says MPIM error occurred, disclose it.\n- Deliver in Telegram-friendly Markdown with bullets, no tables.",
